meta {
  name: Lead For Replacement
  type: http
  seq: 21
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads/for-replacement?policyId={{soldPolicyId}}&reason=cancel_rewrite
  auth: inherit
}

params:query {
  policyId: {{soldPolicyId}}
  reason: cancel_rewrite
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("answers all three questions at once", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("leadId");
    expect(body).to.have.property("householdId");
    expect(body).to.have.property("blockedReason");
  });

  test("a replaceable policy names its household and is not blocked", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    // `soldPolicyId` comes from a policy just written by the Sold form, so it is
    // active, linked to a household and unreplaced.
    expect(body.blockedReason).to.equal(null);
    expect(body.householdId).to.be.a("string");
  });

  test("captures the resume target, if the chain was already started", function () {
    const leadId = res.getBody().leadId;
    if (leadId) bru.setVar("replacementLeadId", leadId);
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /leads/for-replacement — where a replacement should start

  Module `leads` (class-level), permission **`deal_audits:write`**. The
  permission is the *flow's*, not `leads:read`: this is a read, but the only
  thing it is for is deciding a write, and it is the same permission
  `POST /sold-deals` requires — so a caller who passes this can finish the
  chain. Scope comes from `loadOwnedPolicy`, the clamp `PATCH /policies/:id`
  uses, so a policy outside the caller's scope 404s rather than 403s.

  ## Why it exists

  A **Cancel Rewrite** and a **Company Transfer** no longer have endpoints of
  their own. Both run through the ordinary Sold pipeline, which is anchored on a
  lead — so the flow is two forms:

  ```
  policy ─▶ POST /leads (stamped with replacementIntent) ─▶ POST /sold-deals
  ```

  The rep can leave between them. A lead created and then abandoned before the
  Sold form is a real record with no sale on it, and asking again for the same
  action on the same policy must land on *that* lead rather than opening a second
  one beside it. Only the server can answer that, which is what this is for.

  ## Params

  | Param | Req | Notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `policyId` | yes | The policy being replaced. 24-hex. |
  | `reason` | yes | `cancel_rewrite` or `company_transfer`. **No default** — the two apply different money rules (one charges the full premium back, the other charges nothing), so resuming one into the other would be silently wrong. |

  ## Response `200` — three answers, and the caller routes on which it got

  | Shape | Meaning | Where the UI goes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `blockedReason` set | Cannot be replaced: inactive, already replaced, or no household | Nowhere; explain |
  | `leadId` set | A lead was already created and abandoned | `/sold/new?leadId=…` |
  | both null | Nothing started | `/leads/new?replacePolicyId=…&reason=…` |

  ```json
  { "leadId": null, "householdId": "665f…", "blockedReason": null }
  ```

  `blockedReason` is **returned, not thrown**: the caller is asking *whether* it
  may act, and an exception is the wrong shape for "no, and here is why". The
  same guards are enforced for real at lead creation and again at the Sold
  submit — this is the courtesy copy so the chain never starts.

  ## Errors

  | Code | When |
  |---|---|
  | `400` | Missing or malformed `policyId`, or an unrecognised `reason`. |
  | `403` | Caller lacks `deal_audits:write`, or the `leads` module is disabled for the agency. |
  | `404` | No such policy, or it is outside the caller's data scope. |

  ## Run order

  Needs `soldPolicyId` (any active policy linked to a household). Feeds the
  replacement chain: `Create Lead` with a `replacementIntent`, then
  `Create Sold Deal` on the lead it returns.
}
